cite46†5 min read NASA’s SPHEREx Telescope Sees Menagerie of Brown Dwarfs article 6 days ago cite141†5 min read Webb Measures Distance to Farthest Fast Radio Burst, Suggesting Origin article 6 days ago†science.nasa.gov cite151†3 min read NASA Advances LISA Mission Contributions With New Test Telescope article 6 days ago†science.nasa.gov L224: ### Highlights\nL225: \nL226: cite146†3 min read An Impact Structure With Legs article 1 day ago†science.nasa.gov cite152†3 min read NASA’s SpaceX CRS-35 Mission to Launch Health Research article 2 days ago†science.nasa.gov cite147†3 min read Antarctic Sea Ice Grows to Its 2026 Maximum article 2 days ago†science.nasa.gov L227: ### Highlights\nL228: \nL229: cite153†2 min read NASA Model Wing Lights Up During First Pressure Sensitive Paint Tests article 2 weeks ago cite154†2 min read Dr. Natasha Schatzman Receives Vertical Flight Society (VFS) Award article 2 weeks ago cite155†10 min read 2026-2027 DWU: Middle School Design Challenge article 3 weeks ago L230: ### Highlights\nL231: \nL232: cite145†3 min read Hurricane Simon Lurks Off the Mexican Coast article 5 hours ago†science.nasa.gov cite156†3 min read Rains Swamp the Gandak River article 2 weeks ago†science.nasa.gov cite157†1 min read Space Reactors Division article 2 weeks ago L233: ### Featured\nL234: \nL235: cite158†4 min read NASA Demonstrates Next-Generation Heat Shield Technologies article 5 days ago cite159†8 min read NASA Selects Projects to Engage Communities Across the US with NASA Science article 2 weeks ago†science.nasa.gov cite160†3 min read TechRise (for 6–12 graders) article 2 weeks ago L236: ### Featured\nL237: \nL238: cite161†4 min read NASA, Energy Department Advance New Era of Nuclear-Powered Exploration article 6 days ago cite152†3 min read NASA’s SpaceX CRS-35 Mission to Launch Health Research article 2 days ago†science.nasa.gov cite162†2 min read NASA Releases Artemis II Lunar Science Data, Images article 7 days ago L239: ### Highlights\nL240: \nL241: cite163†11 min read La NASA anuncia la cobertura de la misión lunar Artemis II article 7 months ago cite164†15 min read Agenda diaria de la misión a la Luna de Artemis II de la NASA article 7 months ago cite165†6 min read La NASA refuerza Artemis: añade una misión y perfecciona su arquitectura general article 7 months ago L242: \nL243: 20 min read\nL244: # “Houston, We’ve Had a Problem”\nL245: \nL246: John Uri\nL247: \nL248: Johnson Space Center\nL249: \nL250: Apr 13, 2020\nL251: \nL252: Article\nL253: During their third day in space, the crew of Apollo 13, Commander cite166†James A. Lovell , Command Module Pilot cite167†John L. “Jack” Swigert and Lunar Module Pilot cite168†Fred W. Haise , traveling some 205,000 miles from Earth, had just concluded a television broadcast that included views of their Lunar Module (LM) Aquarius and Command Module (CM) Odyssey.\nL254: Controllers in Mission Control at the Manned Spacecraft Center (MSC), now the Johnson Space Center in Houston, enjoyed the broadcast from their consoles, while Lovell’s wife Marilyn, accompanied by cite169†Dr. Charles A. Berry , MSC’s Director of Medical Research and Operations, watched the broadcast from the Visitors Gallery. The astronauts were looking forward to entering lunar orbit in less than day, followed by Lovell and Haise making a landing in the Moon’s Fra Mauro highlands the day after.\nL255: During their 33-hour stay on the Moon, Lovell and Haise planned to complete two spacewalks, conducting geologic surveys and placing scientific instruments that would return data to scientists on the ground long after their departure. Swigert had his own science to conduct from lunar orbit.\nL256: cite170†Image: apollo_13_mcc_tv_transmission_just_before_explosion_w_kranz cite171†Image: apollo_13_marilyn-and-berry_in_mcc_minutes_before_accident L257: Left: Flight Director Kranz (in foreground) watches the Flight Day 3 TV broadcast\nL258: from Apollo 13 just minutes before the accident. Right: Marilyn Lovell (at left) chats\nL259: with Dr. Berry in the MCC Visitors Gallery just minutes before the accident.\nL260: As the astronauts completed the routine housekeeping chores of stirring the cryogenic liquid oxygen and liquid hydrogen in their tanks in the Service Module (SM), they heard a dull bang throughout the docked spacecraft, with Haise in the LM and Lovell and Swigert in the CM.\nL261: At that precise moment, 55 hours 55 minutes and 4 seconds into the flight, Mission Control recorded a sudden shift in the spacecraft’s High Gain Antenna (HGA) communications accompanied by a strange crackle in the otherwise quiet radio stream. Within a few seconds, controllers at several consoles monitoring spacecraft systems began reporting to Flight Director cite172†Eugene F.\nL262: “Gene” Kranz that they were seeing strange data from Apollo 13, including a restart of the main computer, and immediately began troubleshooting the new and unknown problem. Aboard the spacecraft, about 1 to 2 seconds after the bang, the Master Alarm light and the Main Direct Current (DC) B Bus Undervolt light went on, followed by a Fuel Cell light. That prompted Swigert to call Mission Control, “Okay, Houston, we’ve had a problem here.” Capsule communicator (Capcom) astronaut cite173†Jack R.\nL263: Lousma asked, “This is Houston. Say again, please.” Lovell replied, providing some more detail on their condition, “Ah, Houston, we’ve had a problem here. We’ve had a Main B Bus Undervolt.” Lovell reported that sensors picked up a drop in voltage across one of the two main DC electrical buses supplying power to the Command and Service Module (CSM), triggering the warning lights to alert the crew to the problem.\nL264: cite174†Image: apollo_13_fd3_kranz_post_accident_apr_13_1970 cite175†Image: apollo_13_fd3_capcoms_post_accident_apr_13_1970 L265: Left: Flight Director Kranz shortly after the accident. Right: Capcom Lousma\nL266: (seated) assisted by (left to right) Slayton, Mattingly, Stafford and Young,\nL267: shortly after the accident.\nL268: At this point, neither the crew nor the team in Mission Control knew the cause of the problem. Because the voltage on Bus B had come back to normal, everyone’s first inclination was to blame instrumentation or some unknown transient event.\nL269: But Haise, having left Aquarius and now back in Odyssey, radioed down that despite the now normal voltages, there was “a pretty large bang associated with the Caution and Warning there.” The crew first suspected that something had happened to the LM, perhaps a micro-meteoroid strike, and Lovell and Swigert rushed to close the hatch to isolate the two spacecraft.\nL270: Haise noted that the quantity sensor for oxygen tank 2, which had failed earlier in the mission by showing off-scale high and located in the same tank that had experienced anomalies during the cite176†Countdown Demonstration Test back in March, was now oscillating between 20% and 60%, perhaps rocked back to life by the jolt. The crew suddenly faced a cascade of malfunctions.\nL271: First Lovell noted that sensors in the helium tanks used to pressurize the SM’s Reaction Control System (RCS) thrusters were showing alarms as were some of the propellant systems. The DC Main B Bus that showed normal just a few minutes before now showed zero volts, the Alternating Current (AC) bus 2 read zero and the DC Main A Bus showed an undervolt, meaning instead of the normal 27 volts it was reading 25.5 volts.\nL272: Two of the three fuel cells (FCs) that combined oxygen with hydrogen to supply power to the DC busses appeared to be offline, explaining why Main Bus B showed zero volts as it received power from FC 3 and why Main Bus A’s voltage was dropping because it drew power from FC 1 and 2 and FC 1 was offline. Haise attempted to reconnect the fuel cells to get the busses back up but neither FC 1 nor FC 3 appeared to be operational.\nL273: In Mission Control, the team was trying to ascertain if they were dealing with an instrumentation issue or if there was a real problem. Five minutes had elapsed since the crew heard the bang.\nL274: cite177†Image: apollo_13_sm_sector_4_schematic_colorized cite178†Image: apollo_13_sm_electrical_schematic L275: Left: Schematic of the SM tanks and fuel cells. Right: Schematic of the SM Electrical\nL276: Power System at the time of the accident.\nL277: About 14 minutes after the incident began, Lovell called down to say that the quantity in oxygen tank number 2 now read zero, and even more worrisome, “it looks to me, looking out the hatch, that we are venting something. We are venting something out into space. It’s a gas of some sort.” The venting couldn’t be just instrumentation. Kranz got on the internal Mission Control loop to talk to his controllers with some of the most memorable words spoken in human spaceflight:\nL278: “Okay now, let’s everybody keep cool, we got the LM still attached, the LM spacecraft’s good so if we need … to get back home we’ve got a LM to do a good portion of it with. Okay, let’s make sure that we don’t do anything that’s going to blow our CSM electrical power with the batteries or that will cause us to lose the main or the fuel cell number 2. Okay, we want to keep the O2 and that kind of stuff working.\nL279: We’d like to have RCS, but we got the Command Module system, so we’re in good shape if we need to get home. Let’s solve the problem but let’s not make it any worse by guessing.”\nL280: Fifteen minutes had elapsed since the crew heard the bang.\nL281: \nL282: cite179†Image: View of Apollo 13 Flight Director Gene Kranz shortly after the accident cite180†Image: Apollo 13 Flight Director Gene Kranz surveys the MOCR after the accident L283: Two views of Flight Director Kranz shortly after the accident.\nL284: To Mission Control and the crew onboard Apollo 13 several things were becoming clear even as the underlying cause remained unknown. The CSM was losing oxygen required for the crew to breathe and to generate power in the fuel cells. Mission Control estimated that at the rate the quantity was decreasing in oxygen tank 1 they had less than two hours of power generation remaining. The LM appeared unaffected and the idea of using it as a lifeboat started to be discussed.\nL285: The continued venting caused the SM’s RCS thrusters to fire to try to maintain the spacecraft’s attitude, important to maintain good communications. With FC 1 and FC 3 off line, the crew wanted to preserve FC 2 as long as possible. Haise called down to Mission Control that it might be a good idea to think about the consumables available in the LM, even though it could support two crewmembers for two days and now it would have to support three crewmembers for four days.\nL286: Critical consumables included oxygen for the crew, power to run the spacecraft’s systems including the navigation and life support equipment, water both for drinking and to cool the electronic equipment, and carbon dioxide removal.\nL287: Of immediate urgency the crew needed to preserve enough consumables such as electricity and oxygen in the CM to allow for a successful reentry and splashdown at the end of the mission. The two sources of oxygen in the CM were a surge tank and a repress package, the latter a set of three tanks used to repressurize the cabin in case it had to be vented – the crew needed to isolate the surge tank to ensure it retained sufficient oxygen for the crew to breathe during reentry.\nL288: Thinking the leak might be coming from FC 3, Mission Control asked the crew to shut it down – the action ruling out a Moon landing since flight rules stated the spacecraft cannot enter lunar orbit with only two working fuel cells. Shutting down FC 3 had no effect on stopping the leak because the problem was not with any of the fuel cells but with the tanks supplying oxygen to all three of them – tank 2 was empty and tank 1 getting there.\nL289: Less than an hour after the crew heard the bang, Flight Director Kranz instructed his team that the new mission plan involved abandoning the Moon landing, looping around the Moon and getting the crew home safely as quickly as possible. The first order of business was getting back onto a free-return trajectory. A course correction on the mission’s second day specifically maneuvered Apollo 13 from such a path onto a hybrid trajectory to enable the landing at Fra Mauro.\nL290: With the landing off the table, they needed to make another course correction to maneuver back to the free return path. Kranz knew that the SM’s Service Propulsion System (SPS) engine could not be used so he ordered his team to prepare plans to use the LM’s Descent Propulsion System (DPS) engine, normally used to land on the Moon, to perform the maneuver. Fortunately, engineers had previously drawn up contingency plans to use the DPS to maneuver the docked spacecraft in case of an SPS failure.\nL291: cite181†Image: Apollo 13 managers during the crisis cite182†Image: Apollo 13 Flight Director Glynn Lunney looking concerned as he thinks L292: Left: Flight Directors Lunney and Griffin (at left) confer with managers McDivitt and Slayton (at right). Right: Lunney in a thoughtful moment shortly after the accident.\nL293: His shift over, Kranz handed Mission Control over to Flight Director cite183†Glynn S. Lunney and his Black Team of controllers with Lousma staying on as Capcom. Normally the team going off-shift would go home for much needed rest, but this night they stayed and pored over all the available data to understand what happened aboard Apollo 13.\nL294: Lunney began directing his team to come up with procedures to activate the LM Aquarius as quickly as possible to ensure that its communications, navigation and life support systems were all functioning before the CM Odyssey had to be shut down. Lousma called up to the crew that as the quantity in tank 1 “was slowly going down to zero, … we’re starting to think about the LM lifeboat.” Ninety minutes had elapsed since the crisis began.\nL295: cite184†Image: Glynn Lunney after the Apollo 13 accident cite185†Image: Apollo 13 capcom Jack Lousma with Slayton, Mattingly, Brand, and Young L296: Left: Flight Director Lunney (seated) shortly after the accident. Right: Capcom Lousma (seated in white shirt) assisted by (left to right) Slayton, Mattingly, Brand, and Young.\nL297: It was now a race to activate the LM and get all three crewmembers inside Aquarius – Odyssey had just 15 minutes of power left. Haise already in Aquarius radioed to Lousma with the good news, “Jack, I got LM power on.” He performed an abbreviated activation to get oxygen and water flowing, and the electrical, navigation and communications systems enabled. One unexpected communications issue arose – the LM used the same frequency as the spent S-IVB stage, on its way toward an impact with the Moon.\nL298: Under normal circumstances, this interference would not have occurred since the stage would have crashed before the crew activated the LM. Meanwhile, back in Odyssey Swigert busied himself with deactivating that spacecraft, completing the task less than three hours after the crisis began.\nL299: cite186†Image: apollo_13_fd3_press_conference_kraft_mcdivitt_sjoberg_apr_14_1970 cite187†Image: apollo_13_fd3_mcc_engineers_working_the_problem_apr_13_1970 L300: Left: NASA managers (left to right) Kraft, McDivitt and Sjoberg discuss Apollo 13’s condition a few hours after the accident. Right: Controllers in Mission Control work to save the Apollo 13 crew.\nL301: With the immediate crisis of finding the crew a safe haven over, Lunney addressed his team concerning the major issues they all faced – the maneuvers needed to get them home as quickly and safely as possible; reducing power usage in the LM to ensure its batteries lasted as long as possible; and assessing the onboard consumables especially carbon dioxide removal. During a midnight press conference, Deputy Director of the Manned Spacecraft Center, now the Johnson Space Center in Houston, cite188†Christopher C.\nL302: Kraft , cite189†James A. McDivitt , Apollo Spacecraft Program Office Manager, and cite190†Sigurd A.\nL303: “Sig” Sjoberg , Director of Flight Operations, provided reporters with a status of the problems with Apollo 13, with Kraft describing it “as serious situation as we’ve ever had in manned spaceflight.” The managers expressed confidence that the crew would return safely to Earth, although McDivitt added, in comparing the current crisis to the Gemini 8 accident in 1966, that that crew was able to return to Earth in 20 minutes while Apollo 13 had many hours to go.\nL304: cite191†Image: apollo_13_trajectory_corrections_to_get_home cite192†Image: apollo_13_fd5_darkened_cm_during_transearth_coast L305: Left: Apollo 13’s trajectory before and after the accident. Right: Darkened CM\nL306: after its shutdown, the only light coming from the Sun shining through the windows.\nL307: At 61 hours 30 minutes into the mission, at a distance of 216,626 miles and about 5 and a half hours after the accident, the astronauts conducted a 34-second firing of the LM’s DPS engine to put the spacecraft back onto a free-return trajectory. The maneuver raised the altitude of their closest pass around the back side of the Moon from 69 miles to 156 miles. If the crew did nothing else, on this trajectory they would splash down in the Indian Ocean at about 151 hours and 45 minutes.\nL308: This was not an optimal scenario since the prime recovery ship, the USS Iwo Jima (LPH-2), was stationed in the Pacific Ocean and Mission Control wanted them home sooner to ensure adequate consumables remained. A second maneuver after they rounded the Moon, called PC+2 for two hours after pericynthion or closest point to the Moon, would speed up their return and move the landing zone into the Pacific Ocean.\nL309: cite193†Image: apollo_13_post_accident_capcom_brand_w_young cite194†Image: apollo_13_mcc_fd4_kranz_and_griffin_apr_14_1970 L310: Left: Capcom Brand (left) confers with fellow astronaut Young (right).\nL311: Right: Flight Directors Kranz (left), Griffin (right) and Lunney (just visible at far left).\nL312: In Mission Control, cite195†Joseph P. Kerwin took over the Capcom position from Lousma and Flight Director cite196†Gerald D. “Gerry” Griffin ’s Gold Team took their seats at their consoles. For a short time, backup Lunar Module Pilot cite197†Charles M. Duke , recovered from his bout of cite198†German measles , took over the Capcom position, followed by cite199†Vance D. Brand .\nL313: For the next few hours as the astronauts continued to approach the Moon, they continued conserving power in Aquarius and making preparations for the PC+2 burn. Kerwin advised them to move the lithium hydroxide canisters, used to scrub the cabin’s atmosphere of carbon dioxide (CO2), from Odyssey to Aquarius. A buildup of condensate in the cold CM cabin could render the canisters unusable. The crew entered a rest period although sleep was understandably hard to come by.\nL314: Shortly before Apollo 13 entered the Moon’s shadow, Kranz’s White Team of controllers arrived back in MCC to take their consoles, just 18 hours after the accident.\nL315: cite200†Image: apollo_13_as-13-60-8636_keeler cite201†Image: apollo_13_as13-60-8637_tsiolkovsky cite202†Image: apollo_13_as-13-60-8648_mare_muscoviense L316: Three images from Apollo 13 as they round the farside of the Moon.\nL317: Left: Keeler Crater. Middle: Tsiolkovsky Crater. Right: Mare Muscoviense.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHedonism (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy) (https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hedonism/)\nciteturn5view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hedonism/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 309\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChange Blindness (https://isle.hanover.edu/isle3/Ch09Attention/Ch09ChangeBlindness.html)\nciteturn5view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://isle.hanover.edu/isle3/Ch09Attention/Ch09ChangeBlindness.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 16\n"
